<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  W3C SCXML 5.10 + B.2: an event a HOST injects carries a payload, and the
  document reads it as a value rather than as text.

  This is the one direction nothing in the repository asked. Every integration
  fixture drives its machine with `raise_external(event, "", "")` — measured
  2026-08-16, the payload argument is the empty string in every call on every
  channel — so the whole host-to-datamodel edge was covered by nothing. The
  W3C suite does not reach it either: its payloads originate INSIDE the
  document (`<send><content>`, `<param>`, `<donedata>`), which is a different
  code path that each backend implements separately.

  It is also the edge a supervising host actually uses. `examples/ai_loop`
  answers its machine with `{"done":true}` and the document selects on
  `_event.data.done`; the same shape reaches SCE from any embedder that has a
  JSON payload and a state machine to give it to.

  Five claims, and a `<final>` for each failure so a red says which one broke
  rather than only that something did:

    settled    all five
    mangled    the JSON payload did not become addressable properties
    garbled    the plain-text payload did not arrive as that string
    evaluated  `2 + 3` arrived as 5 — the payload was RUN
    flattened  an XML payload did not become a DOM
    swallowed  a payload that OPENS like XML but is not a document did not
               reach the document at all

  The last two are §scxml-B-2-8-1's XML rung and the sentence that closes the
  clause, and they are one question asked twice: the rung is conditioned on
  the content being a document — "if the Processor can interpret the content
  as a valid XML document, it MUST create the corresponding DOM structure" —
  and what happens when it is not is a MUST of its own, "Otherwise, the
  Processor MUST treat the content as a space-normalized string literal".
  Measured 2026-08-19, nine script engines gave four different answers, and
  every one of the four was some backend's answer to `flattened` or
  `swallowed`: Rust and Go answered nil, the two C++ engines built a DOM out
  of whatever fragment happened to parse (`XMLDocument::isValid` asked whether
  the tree was non-empty rather than whether the parse succeeded), and C11 had
  no XML rung on the event path at all, so it answered a string where its six
  siblings answered a document.

  It is not a corner. Every `error.*` event this repository raises carries a
  message that names the SCXML construct that failed — `<assign> to detail
  failed` — so every platform error opens with `<` and is not a document. The
  field was filled in at 192 raise sites in the round before this one, and
  three backends delivered nothing.

  The third is the one that separates reading the payload from running it,
  and it is written to be decisive in both languages this datamodel is
  lowered into: `2 + 3` is an expression in Lua and in JavaScript alike, and
  evaluating it gives 5 in both. §scxml-B-2-8-1 gives the payload three
  readings — XML becomes a DOM, JSON becomes the value, anything else becomes
  a space-normalized string — and text that merely looks like arithmetic is
  the third of those. Measured 2026-08-17, five of six backends took a fourth
  reading: they ran the text first, so this event landed in `evaluated` on
  Rust, Go, Python, C11 and Kotlin's QuickJS engine, and in `settled` on cpp
  and Kotlin's Rhino. One document, two answers, and no test asked either.

  The first guard tests `_event.data` before indexing it. That is not defensive
  style: a backend that drops the payload leaves the variable nil, and
  `nil.milestone` raises where `undefined.milestone` would — the run would then
  be reporting an evaluation error rather than the missing payload, and W3C
  §5.9.1 (a cond that raises is false) would send it to the same place with a
  different reason. Short-circuiting keeps the answer about the payload.

  `hold the line` starts with a letter on purpose: cpp `EventDataHelper::
  jsonStringToScriptValue` attempts a JSON parse only for bodies that open
  with a JSON-eligible character, so a word-initial payload exercises the
  raw-string fallback rather than the parser.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml" version="1.0"
       name="event_data_arrives_as_sent" datamodel="ecmascript" initial="waiting">

  <state id="waiting">
    <transition event="payload"
                cond="_event.data &amp;&amp; _event.data.milestone === 'refined' &amp;&amp; _event.data.turns === 2"
                target="heard"/>
    <transition event="payload" target="mangled"/>
  </state>

  <state id="heard">
    <transition event="note" cond="_event.data === 'hold the line'" target="quoted"/>
    <transition event="note" target="garbled"/>
  </state>

  <state id="quoted">
    <transition event="arith" cond="_event.data === '2 + 3'" target="documented"/>
    <transition event="arith" target="evaluated"/>
  </state>

  <!-- §scxml-B-2-8-1's XML rung, reached through the EVENT path. The
       `<data>` path is `xml_data_is_a_dom_tree`'s, and it says in its own
       comment that it leaves this one alone; the two rungs are lowered on
       separate code in every backend, and this is the half nothing asked.
       `nodeName` rather than `getElementsByTagName`, because a pair of
       methods is what every backend supplied when the surface was last
       measured — the document's own vocabulary is what proves a DOM. -->
  <state id="documented">
    <transition event="doc"
                cond="_event.data &amp;&amp; _event.data.documentElement &amp;&amp; _event.data.documentElement.nodeName === 'books'"
                target="opening"/>
    <transition event="doc" target="flattened"/>
  </state>

  <!-- The sentence that closes the clause. A leading `<` is a GUESS about
       which reading applies, and this payload is what the guess gets wrong:
       it opens like a document and is not one, which is the shape of every
       `error.*` message the engine raises. The comparison is against the
       SPACE-NORMALIZED text, so a backend that fell through by handing the
       bytes over untouched is not passing either — normalization is a
       reading, not a passthrough. -->
  <state id="opening">
    <transition event="broken" cond="_event.data === '&lt;assign&gt; to detail failed'" target="settled"/>
    <transition event="broken" target="swallowed"/>
  </state>

  <final id="settled"/>
  <final id="mangled"/>
  <final id="garbled"/>
  <final id="evaluated"/>
  <final id="flattened"/>
  <final id="swallowed"/>
</scxml>
